1
00:00:18,242 --> 00:00:23,931
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a sutta maybe for monks, really, a discussion for monks.

2
00:00:24,491 --> 00:00:26,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe they would understand it a bit more.

3
00:00:27,316 --> 00:00:30,260
[SPEAKER_00]: But in any case, it's called One in Training.

4
00:00:32,904 --> 00:00:33,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha says,

5
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:50,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Amongst this recitation of more than 150 training rules, he means the Patimokkha, comes up every fortnight in reference to which sons of good families desiring the goal train themselves.

6
00:00:52,248 --> 00:00:56,570
[SPEAKER_00]: There are these three trainings under which all that is gathered.

7
00:00:56,610 --> 00:00:57,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Which three?

8
00:00:57,650 --> 00:01:02,932
[SPEAKER_00]: The training in heightened virtue, the training in heightened mind, and the training in heightened discernment.

9
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:08,534
[SPEAKER_00]: These are the three trainings under which all that is gathered.

10
00:01:10,215 --> 00:01:16,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So there is a case where a monk is wholly accomplished, fully accomplished in virtue,

11
00:01:17,688 --> 00:01:18,789
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not ignoring.

12
00:01:18,809 --> 00:01:18,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

13
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:20,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

14
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:21,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I...

15
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:21,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

16
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

17
00:01:42,261 --> 00:01:47,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I have not declared that to be a disqualification in these circumstances.

18
00:01:47,771 --> 00:01:52,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, being wholly accomplished in virtue doesn't disqualify.

19
00:01:54,213 --> 00:02:03,440
[SPEAKER_00]: But as for the training rules that are basic to the holy life and proper to the holy life, he's one of permanent virtue, one of steadfast virtue.

20
00:02:04,481 --> 00:02:08,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Having undertaken them, he trains in reference to the training rules.

21
00:02:08,644 --> 00:02:17,932
[SPEAKER_00]: And with the ending of the first three fetters, he's a stream-winner, a sotāpanna, never again destined for the states of woe, certain, headed for self-awakening.

22
00:02:18,512 --> 00:02:23,336
[SPEAKER_00]: And it goes on through that, through the different levels of attainment, all the way up to Arahant Shastri.

23
00:02:23,597 --> 00:02:24,337
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of that.

24
00:02:24,377 --> 00:02:24,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

25
00:02:24,778 --> 00:02:28,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Accomplished in... fully accomplished in virtue, fully accomplished in...

26
00:02:29,302 --> 00:02:29,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind.

27
00:02:29,582 --> 00:02:29,902
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

28
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:30,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind.

29
00:02:30,263 --> 00:02:30,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Heightened mind.

30
00:02:30,923 --> 00:02:32,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Heightened discernment.

31
00:02:32,785 --> 00:02:33,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that would follow.

32
00:02:34,306 --> 00:02:35,807
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense that the, you know, the...

33
00:02:36,548 --> 00:02:40,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And at the end of that sutta, he says, those who are partially accomplished attain a path.

34
00:02:40,670 --> 00:02:41,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Those who are whole, fully accomplished,

35
00:02:54,648 --> 00:02:58,650
[SPEAKER_00]: The training rules, I tell you, are not in vain.

36
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:00,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

37
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:03,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the important part.

38
00:03:04,232 --> 00:03:07,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The rules that are essence of the training are basically the eight precepts.

39
00:03:10,691 --> 00:03:24,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever the Guru would talk about, you know, the wise men of the past, the virtuous people of the past, it's it's the eight precepts are these principles, but then you can have many other rules in in in in the in the monastic vinaya, you have many other rules, but they're all kind of within that.

40
00:03:24,815 --> 00:03:25,855
[SPEAKER_00]: The one, the rules that matter.

41
00:03:26,035 --> 00:03:28,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you have the circumstantial, social rules and so on, minor rules.

42
00:03:29,616 --> 00:03:35,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But the ones that matter for the training are basically the ones that are around lust, aversion, distraction and delusion.

43
00:03:35,558 --> 00:03:35,898
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

44
00:03:37,103 --> 00:03:43,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, that's meant for the monks, but actually even a lay person, if they become accomplished in eight precepts, can expect the same.

45
00:03:45,832 --> 00:03:48,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Heightened mind and heightened discernment.

46
00:03:48,836 --> 00:03:56,724
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, many lay people in the suttas were anāgāmīs, they were teaching monks, the new monks, because they were so developed.

47
00:03:56,924 --> 00:03:59,107
[SPEAKER_01]: And, so what is that development?

48
00:03:59,888 --> 00:04:04,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it the technical letter of the rule, you know, prescribed by the contemporary tradition?

49
00:04:05,389 --> 00:04:05,749
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

50
00:04:05,849 --> 00:04:07,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is it?

51
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:09,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that accomplishment?

52
00:04:10,010 --> 00:04:11,111
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it make you accomplished?

53
00:04:12,411 --> 00:04:15,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it knowing the letter of every rule and looking for every technicality?

54
00:04:16,272 --> 00:04:20,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Or knowing the state of mind and intention behind your actions, which is closer?

55
00:04:21,785 --> 00:04:35,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever people are latching onto technicalities, and the latter of the rules, it's when the intention for the state of mind is either obscured, not taking responsibility for, or blatantly just to be avoided.

56
00:04:37,307 --> 00:04:46,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't feel responsible, because this is the tradition that I follow, this is the rules that I follow, this is the letter that I follow, and I don't think...

57
00:04:46,492 --> 00:04:47,873
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to think for myself.

58
00:04:48,613 --> 00:04:58,878
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you can follow such rules in such manner for a hundred years, have, like, the full ordination, the highest approval, and still not be accomplished in virtue.

59
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:02,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what Śrīla Prabhupāda says.

60
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:05,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Taking on virtue,

61
00:05:07,006 --> 00:05:13,991
[SPEAKER_01]: As a sense of external duty, for which you're not allowing yourself to feel responsible for, internally.

62
00:05:15,372 --> 00:05:18,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And why would a person not allow themselves to feel responsible for that?

63
00:05:18,474 --> 00:05:21,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the moment you become responsible, you become liable to doubt.

64
00:05:23,037 --> 00:05:27,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's exactly why Sīla Bhāgavata Paramāsa is bundled with the factor of doubt.

65
00:05:27,761 --> 00:05:28,761
[SPEAKER_01]: If you actually manage

66
00:05:29,690 --> 00:05:39,613
[SPEAKER_01]: To break yourself away from the dependence on the external letter and take responsibility for the virtue, you will creating a basis for overcoming the factor of doubt.

67
00:05:40,793 --> 00:05:47,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't allow yourself to step away from it in that correct sense, in that correct direction, you will never overcome doubt.

68
00:05:47,895 --> 00:05:53,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the reason why the duty is imposed by yourself to yourself is so the doubt cannot arise.

69
00:05:54,173 --> 00:05:55,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And you often see that.

70
00:05:55,854 --> 00:06:02,998
[SPEAKER_01]: When a mind that's dependent on the letter, somebody points out that that letter might be wrong, they're overwhelmed.

71
00:06:03,098 --> 00:06:05,539
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we witness it many times.

72
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:07,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Overwhelmed with the existential doubt.

73
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:14,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they realize everything leading up to that was based on that letter saying what it says, so I didn't feel responsible for.

74
00:06:14,204 --> 00:06:15,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet now, somebody just questions that.

75
00:06:16,665 --> 00:06:19,546
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't even prove that the letter of your rule is wrong.

76
00:06:19,967 --> 00:06:21,087
[SPEAKER_01]: They just start questioning it.

77
00:06:21,447 --> 00:06:22,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are shaking.

78
00:06:24,439 --> 00:06:30,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that doesn't concern you, means you lack even basic authenticity, basic self-awareness.

79
00:06:30,707 --> 00:06:32,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why there is no right view for you.

80
00:06:35,053 --> 00:06:38,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to understand that, that yes, you learned the letter of the rules,

81
00:06:39,823 --> 00:06:44,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But, depending how, like, how far you're gonna go into the letter of the rules, and why?

82
00:06:45,686 --> 00:06:47,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because I'm not really still understanding the rule?

83
00:06:47,927 --> 00:06:48,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, go further.

84
00:06:49,327 --> 00:06:55,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it, no, I understand the rule, but I want more technicalities to prevent me from myself and from my own mind?

85
00:06:56,150 --> 00:06:57,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you want to avoid the practice.

86
00:06:58,131 --> 00:07:00,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where samādhi is, as we said before.

87
00:07:01,472 --> 00:07:05,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that want to overwhelm the mind, the monk doesn't let the mind being overwhelmed.

88
00:07:05,514 --> 00:07:07,095
[SPEAKER_01]: That's his practice of samādhi.

89
00:07:08,426 --> 00:07:14,770
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you understood the rule, you understand the 8 precepts, you understand the 10 precepts, you understand 150 training rules.

90
00:07:16,191 --> 00:07:19,293
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not clear about some rule, you clarify it, that's it.

91
00:07:20,894 --> 00:07:22,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you practice.

92
00:07:23,836 --> 00:07:32,822
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice in a way by keep making sure when you're about to act, you act according with those rules, or you act according with non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

93
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:41,848
[SPEAKER_01]: If you break it, you see where you broke it, you see why you broke it, and you don't need to depend on technicality, because you're always aware of your intention behind the actions.

94
00:07:42,869 --> 00:07:46,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if somebody points out technicality allows you this, but it's like, it doesn't.

95
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:49,156
[SPEAKER_01]: The state of mind doesn't allow you to do that.

96
00:07:49,836 --> 00:07:55,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you actually stop looking, if you start looking at the context of the rules, eight precepts or 150 precepts,

97
00:07:56,830 --> 00:08:03,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And maintain the context of the rules in your mind, there will be no loopholes for you, and there will be no need for the loopholes.

98
00:08:04,672 --> 00:08:16,315
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start looking at the letter that gets proliferated more and more and more into this definition of a sub-definition of a sub-definition, then, all of it, it will be about loopholes.

99
00:08:17,296 --> 00:08:25,558
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be about things that are left not addressed, and you know they come under the same context, but you have drifted away from that context so much now,

100
00:08:26,587 --> 00:08:29,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Into these particulars, that you lost the sight of the forest, pretty much.

101
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:34,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly when people and monks become technical.

102
00:08:34,492 --> 00:08:36,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, technically, I'm not breaking the rule and stuff.

103
00:08:36,593 --> 00:08:40,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, what is the state of mind behind that action?

104
00:08:40,415 --> 00:08:41,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You are breaking the virtue.

105
00:08:42,557 --> 00:08:43,117
[SPEAKER_01]: End of story.

106
00:08:43,837 --> 00:08:48,120
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no accomplishment in virtue, unless you take the virtue on that level of the mind.

107
00:08:50,167 --> 00:08:55,769
[SPEAKER_01]: But people confuse learning about the rules, learning about technicalities, for keeping the rules and keeping the technicalities.

108
00:08:55,869 --> 00:09:08,073
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, they feel justified in not taking it personally, in not taking responsibility for it, but projecting and keeping responsibility on the level of, well, that's the tradition, that's the authority, that's how it's been for hundreds of years.

109
00:09:08,113 --> 00:09:08,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

110
00:09:09,193 --> 00:09:14,934
[SPEAKER_01]: You can choose to accept the tradition, the traditional rules, the traditional standards.

111
00:09:15,234 --> 00:09:15,814
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all fine.

112
00:09:16,294 --> 00:09:19,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if you do so, you still have to recognize that you're responsible for that.

113
00:09:20,775 --> 00:09:29,257
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for either choosing to interpret things for yourself, for either choosing to follow this commentary, for either choosing to follow someone else's interpretation or someone else's tradition.

114
00:09:29,397 --> 00:09:29,977
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on you.

115
00:09:30,257 --> 00:09:32,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing justifies you in choosing that.

116
00:09:33,417 --> 00:09:36,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you need to recognize that, eh...

117
00:09:37,762 --> 00:09:45,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you you understand it for yourself directly, or whether you follow someone else's interpretation, you need to know why you are doing it.

118
00:09:46,350 --> 00:09:47,891
[SPEAKER_01]: That needs to be clear to you.

119
00:09:48,332 --> 00:09:50,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I ask you, oh, do you keep the eight precepts?

120
00:09:50,614 --> 00:09:50,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

121
00:09:50,854 --> 00:09:51,975
[SPEAKER_01]: What about the fifth precept?

122
00:09:51,995 --> 00:09:52,976
[SPEAKER_01]: What's your standard on it?

123
00:09:53,016 --> 00:09:55,278
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you understand such and such circumstance?

124
00:09:55,298 --> 00:09:57,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And you tell me, well, I follow the tradition of such and such teacher.

125
00:09:58,721 --> 00:10:00,062
[SPEAKER_01]: I, as a dog, that's fine.

126
00:10:00,563 --> 00:10:00,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

127
00:10:02,525 --> 00:10:04,285
[SPEAKER_01]: You should be able to tell me why do you follow that.

128
00:10:04,926 --> 00:10:16,409
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, how is that way of like following that particular interpretation or the rule or take on the rule, how is that feeding your accomplishment in virtue directly?

129
00:10:16,469 --> 00:10:17,609
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that connected to it?

130
00:10:18,329 --> 00:10:20,870
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just say I follow because he's a great teacher.

131
00:10:21,671 --> 00:10:24,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, but what is the benefit of following that?

132
00:10:24,094 --> 00:10:24,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see that for yourself?

133
00:10:25,777 --> 00:10:29,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you're just going by mere reputation and mere tradition and hearsay?

134
00:10:30,383 --> 00:10:35,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, you know, the Buddha said many times, something you shouldn't really do, unless you're very unwise.

135
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:46,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, follow, like, you know, clarify whatever else, but never feel justified on just, oh, well, I belong to this great tradition now, and these are our standards, and that's the end of the game there.

136
00:10:46,917 --> 00:10:47,157
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

137
00:10:48,097 --> 00:10:48,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

138
00:10:48,897 --> 00:10:50,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I keep these standards?

139
00:10:50,758 --> 00:10:54,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I keep them authentically, individually, personally, internally?

140
00:10:55,220 --> 00:11:00,862
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, I don't need the tradition and the standards, because it's kept on this level, not on the level of traditional behavior.

141
00:11:01,689 --> 00:11:03,029
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how exactly what was said.

142
00:11:03,369 --> 00:11:18,874
[SPEAKER_01]: If you actually accomplish yourself on that level, in that virtue, where you're not shying away from doubt, but willing to endure it, clarifying the context, maintaining the context, not proliferating the rule to prevent you from having doubt arise in your mind again, or I will never do that again.

143
00:11:18,894 --> 00:11:20,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait, but it wasn't an offense.

144
00:11:20,594 --> 00:11:21,154
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not an issue.

145
00:11:21,434 --> 00:11:23,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't even rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

146
00:11:24,395 --> 00:11:25,296
[SPEAKER_01]: But why am I doubting it?

147
00:11:25,316 --> 00:11:26,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what doubt is.

148
00:11:26,597 --> 00:11:31,740
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an irrational mind, a hindrance, that tries to throw you away, tries to make you act out of it.

149
00:11:33,081 --> 00:11:38,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And here you are, acting out of it, because now you're redefining and making new rules, so you will never do stuff that you know was nothing wrong with.

150
00:11:39,891 --> 00:11:43,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because you doubted on account of it, and you never... you don't know how to deal with the doubt.

151
00:11:43,772 --> 00:11:51,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by taking the virtue wrongly, you will not be able to deal with the doubt, because of which you keep taking the virtue wrongly, because of which you can't deal with the doubt.

152
00:11:51,274 --> 00:11:52,034
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no samādhi.

153
00:11:52,054 --> 00:11:53,534
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no... No samādhi, exactly.

154
00:11:53,614 --> 00:11:56,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely no samādhi, because there is no accomplishment.

155
00:11:56,335 --> 00:12:02,877
[SPEAKER_01]: If you take the virtue rightly, endure the doubt, don't act out of the doubt, sustain the context of the virtue... No.

156
00:12:02,897 --> 00:12:03,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know.

157
00:12:04,329 --> 00:12:12,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what it is, but the mind pressures you to find out more, look into technique, create this, create that, act left, act right, avoid, prevent.

158
00:12:13,173 --> 00:12:17,015
[SPEAKER_01]: If you sustain that and not act out of it, you will become accomplished in the virtue.

159
00:12:17,115 --> 00:12:18,276
[SPEAKER_01]: And basically, how did you do that?

160
00:12:18,416 --> 00:12:20,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's already the practice of samādhi.

161
00:12:20,477 --> 00:12:22,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Not moving in regard to the mind that wants to move you.

162
00:12:23,578 --> 00:12:24,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said.

163
00:12:24,458 --> 00:12:29,641
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, if somebody is accomplished, at least in that basis, three factors will have to be destroyed.

164
00:12:30,853 --> 00:12:35,376
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not be a sotāpanna if you have taken virtue on that level and endured it.

165
00:12:37,117 --> 00:12:45,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will certainly not be a sotāpanna if you've taken virtue on the level of the external letter of the law, the rule, the tradition, the authority, the ājā, the senior monk, whatever else.

166
00:12:46,907 --> 00:12:54,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they were right, by the way, in the standards that they laid upon you, if you took it externally, there is no basis for your virtue to actually take place.

167
00:12:55,472 --> 00:13:03,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, if you... the first precept of not harming, not killing a living being, why do you keep that?

168
00:13:03,656 --> 00:13:04,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that wrong?

169
00:13:04,457 --> 00:13:05,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't know why,

170
00:13:06,738 --> 00:13:08,019
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be accomplished in it.

171
00:13:08,099 --> 00:13:10,841
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just kind of circumstantially taken that external rule.

172
00:13:11,782 --> 00:13:13,363
[SPEAKER_01]: You learn them circumstantially.

173
00:13:14,544 --> 00:13:19,387
[SPEAKER_01]: You investigate them circumstantially, but once you start keeping them, no, you need to know for yourself.

174
00:13:19,467 --> 00:13:22,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this needs to be seen personally.

175
00:13:22,830 --> 00:13:24,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why this is not done?

176
00:13:24,431 --> 00:13:25,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Why it's not good to do this?

177
00:13:26,412 --> 00:13:27,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

178
00:13:27,493 --> 00:13:29,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see what is unwholesome, what is wholesome.

179
00:13:30,115 --> 00:13:31,276
[SPEAKER_00]: That is your discernment thing.

180
00:13:31,777 --> 00:13:33,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Well... And the clearer that is... That's what you're going to start seeing.

181
00:13:33,838 --> 00:13:39,764
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to start seeing wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome, because you're seeing the root of that which makes it unwholesome, what makes it wholesome.

182
00:13:40,184 --> 00:13:40,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

183
00:13:40,544 --> 00:13:47,530
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will not see that if you just block it and say, well, this is our tradition, this is my culture, this is what a Buddhist person does.

184
00:13:47,550 --> 00:13:47,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

185
00:13:47,911 --> 00:13:48,972
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, okay.

186
00:13:49,852 --> 00:13:50,513
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's

187
00:13:53,412 --> 00:13:55,933
[SPEAKER_01]: That level of, oh, I am such and such.

188
00:13:55,953 --> 00:13:58,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I identify with that religion, with that observance, with that rule.

189
00:13:58,933 --> 00:13:59,353
[SPEAKER_01]: I keep it.

190
00:13:59,853 --> 00:14:00,493
[SPEAKER_01]: No questions asked.

191
00:14:01,054 --> 00:14:01,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:14:01,914 --> 00:14:02,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it makes you feel safe.

193
00:14:03,134 --> 00:14:03,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, yeah.

194
00:14:03,994 --> 00:14:05,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, oh, questioning means you don't want to keep it.

195
00:14:05,855 --> 00:14:06,035
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

196
00:14:06,435 --> 00:14:08,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Questioning means I want to know what I keep.

197
00:14:09,775 --> 00:14:14,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no, there's no justification for you assuming cynical reasons for my questioning.

198
00:14:15,516 --> 00:14:17,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless it's your own cynical mind putting that over there.

199
00:14:18,742 --> 00:14:18,862
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

200
00:14:19,042 --> 00:14:20,603
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to understand what I keep.

201
00:14:21,483 --> 00:14:27,066
[SPEAKER_01]: But for many people, even questioning to understand what will make you... like, do I need to keep this?

202
00:14:27,126 --> 00:14:27,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I want to keep this?

203
00:14:28,106 --> 00:14:28,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I want to be a monk?

204
00:14:28,927 --> 00:14:29,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I want to practice?

205
00:14:30,387 --> 00:14:33,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something also they... it makes you unpleasant, makes you doubtful.

206
00:14:33,809 --> 00:14:34,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you refuse to think about it.

207
00:14:35,290 --> 00:14:36,230
[SPEAKER_01]: You refuse to question.

208
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:37,571
[SPEAKER_01]: You just double down.

209
00:14:37,911 --> 00:14:38,811
[SPEAKER_01]: This is my duty.

210
00:14:38,991 --> 00:14:39,852
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the tradition.

211
00:14:39,932 --> 00:14:42,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And you double down on your own sīlavāta paramāsa.

212
00:14:43,045 --> 00:14:46,510
[SPEAKER_01]: If you look at the beginning of the monastic order, there were no rules.

213
00:14:59,068 --> 00:14:59,868
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course.

214
00:15:00,108 --> 00:15:00,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma was clear.

215
00:15:01,189 --> 00:15:02,329
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not obscured.

216
00:15:02,649 --> 00:15:05,870
[SPEAKER_01]: People knew exactly what behaviors were in line with it and what behaviors weren't.

217
00:15:06,090 --> 00:15:13,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so... and that... that... that basic... you can boil it down to... don't do anything unwholesome.

218
00:15:13,492 --> 00:15:15,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Know what unwholesome is and don't do it.

219
00:15:15,492 --> 00:15:26,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why also, as the thing sort of progressed, back in the Buddhist time, that whole need for, like, you know, a new monk would have to spend, like, five years training with senior monk.

220
00:15:27,453 --> 00:15:29,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody that accepted Him.

221
00:15:30,035 --> 00:15:35,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, an untrained mind will start, like, you know, will not be able to necessarily even sustain the right context.

222
00:15:35,739 --> 00:15:56,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to spend the first few years learning that context, but you don't want to spend the first few years just learning the letter of some tradition that you got into, without actually understanding why you're doing it, or developing that authentic recognition that it's still up to you whether you keep it or not, and to what extent, and you want to see the intentions behind your actions there.

223
00:15:57,856 --> 00:16:12,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's often like, okay, so, basically, even that process of learning for five years from a senior monk has become like a mechanical letter of stuff you go through, without actually learning, and without monk teaching you either.

224
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:26,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just for five years, you're under someone's, like, tutorship, but in reality, it's just basically learning of that external form, external letter,

225
00:16:27,895 --> 00:16:43,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Not being taught responsibility behind, not being taught how to see the responsibility behind, and how to take it on, in which case you would... you wouldn't even need, then, the further definitions of these rules, because you would be unable to ignore the unwholesome intentions before you act, so you wouldn't act.

226
00:16:44,208 --> 00:16:45,009
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the training.

227
00:16:46,550 --> 00:16:50,413
[SPEAKER_01]: But even training has become like a... like a traditional form.

228
00:16:52,354 --> 00:16:56,738
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, external, on a level of sīlabhāta paramāsa, on the level of virtue.

229
00:17:00,093 --> 00:17:00,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, yeah.

230
00:17:00,754 --> 00:17:03,515
[SPEAKER_01]: It's it's it's just it's like a dead matter in that sense, yeah.

231
00:17:04,356 --> 00:17:09,138
[SPEAKER_01]: But, individually, everybody can rekindle it for themselves, if they actually exactly studied in the suttas.

232
00:17:10,431 --> 00:17:19,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Reading the origin stories and so on, and you start seeing... you get... you get in that livable context, and you know, it's about the state of mind, it's about the perception, how I perceive it, what I choose to do.

233
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:31,291
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about, you know, commentary of a sub-commentary of a sub-commentary defining this is allowable, and then another commentary defining that is not allowable, and me now spending nights and figuring out who might be more right than these two.

234
00:17:31,372 --> 00:17:31,532
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

235
00:17:32,112 --> 00:17:33,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is my intention rooted?

236
00:17:33,353 --> 00:17:34,314
[SPEAKER_01]: You got your answer.

237
00:17:35,207 --> 00:17:43,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And on top of that, not just that you got the answer as to what is the right thing to do, you also started accomplishing yourself in virtue, which is a basis for satipaṭṭhāna.

238
00:17:46,573 --> 00:17:52,916
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't have the right view, if you have not reached the fruits of stream-entry, it's because you haven't been accomplished in virtue.

239
00:17:54,257 --> 00:17:56,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's a good news, because now you know what to do.

240
00:17:56,798 --> 00:17:57,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Accomplish yourself in virtue.

241
00:17:58,779 --> 00:17:59,379
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you do that?

242
00:17:59,779 --> 00:18:00,840
[SPEAKER_01]: By taking it personally.

243
00:18:02,203 --> 00:18:03,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning the rules, sure.

244
00:18:03,844 --> 00:18:05,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning the interpretation, sure.

245
00:18:05,584 --> 00:18:12,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But not relying on the word of the rule, the authority of interpretation or tradition, as this is why it's kept.

246
00:18:12,567 --> 00:18:12,687
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

247
00:18:12,868 --> 00:18:14,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kept because I choose to keep it.

248
00:18:14,988 --> 00:18:20,911
[SPEAKER_01]: And I choose to keep it because I see direct relationship between that and wholesome mind, or not that and unwholesome mind.

249
00:18:22,993 --> 00:18:29,456
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will inevitably, initially, certainly, bring the doubt, bring that sense of, oh, so I could just decide to not keep...

250
00:18:29,536 --> 00:18:30,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

251
00:18:30,656 --> 00:18:33,638
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're gonna feel the weight of keeping that.

252
00:18:34,158 --> 00:18:35,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you become accomplished.

253
00:18:36,799 --> 00:18:43,842
[SPEAKER_00]: But in also doing that, you will eventually begin to see which rules are actually obsolete.

254
00:18:44,644 --> 00:18:46,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but you wouldn't really worry about that.

255
00:18:46,307 --> 00:18:48,330
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't, but you would, as the Buddha said...

256
00:18:48,350 --> 00:18:49,853
[SPEAKER_00]: It would become self-evident, exactly.

257
00:18:49,873 --> 00:18:50,394
[SPEAKER_00]: The minor rules...

258
00:18:50,414 --> 00:18:52,898
[SPEAKER_01]: The minor rules will not affect him, and he will know that.

259
00:18:53,219 --> 00:18:54,761
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd still confess, he wouldn't have an issue.

260
00:18:54,781 --> 00:18:55,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

261
00:18:57,572 --> 00:19:14,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes the problem is, if a person is not accomplished in the virtue, and then get a sense of righteousness, of discovering of this, what we are saying, and then they just try to form a new tradition of dismissing all the traditions and interpreting things for yourself, in a same external manner of sīlabāta paramāsa.

262
00:19:14,865 --> 00:19:15,966
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to change anything.

263
00:19:16,647 --> 00:19:20,630
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to take things personally, and then what needs to be changed will happen by itself.

264
00:19:21,645 --> 00:19:28,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have the authentic mind, you have the accomplishment in virtue, samādhi is there, what change occurs will be the right change.

265
00:19:28,926 --> 00:19:30,046
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have to do it.

266
00:19:30,567 --> 00:19:31,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't have to act out of it.

267
00:19:32,247 --> 00:19:44,989
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you would naturally drift away from things that are unsuitable, traditions that are unsuitable, practices that are unsuitable, into the directions that are suitable for the right view, for this passion and for everything else.

268
00:19:45,049 --> 00:19:47,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Without needing to make a big deal out of it.

269
00:19:48,810 --> 00:19:50,890
[SPEAKER_00]: But you wouldn't become this self-righteous.

270
00:19:51,695 --> 00:19:53,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible, impossible, impossible.

271
00:19:53,936 --> 00:19:55,277
[SPEAKER_01]: You would not either become scared.

272
00:19:56,617 --> 00:20:03,480
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be self-righteous trying to change everybody, but if everybody comes after you in account of it, you wouldn't shy away, because you know what's right.

273
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:11,484
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wouldn't need to change the established external norms, but you wouldn't be afraid to challenge them either, because it's not rooted in your conceit.

274
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in recognition of what the accomplishment of virtue is and the right view.

275
00:20:17,143 --> 00:20:23,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to question those things, you wouldn't just, you know, be on a crusade to change the... as I said, but if it comes up to it, you wouldn't be afraid of it.

276
00:20:27,952 --> 00:20:32,436
[SPEAKER_01]: If circumstances bring it up, you will have no issues dealing with it and addressing it.

277
00:20:34,517 --> 00:20:40,042
[SPEAKER_00]: In... there's another sutta, Samana Pāla Sutta.

278
00:20:41,658 --> 00:20:51,838
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha says, a mendicant, a monk, thus accomplished in virtue, sees no danger in any quarter in regards to their virtuous restraint.

279
00:20:54,959 --> 00:20:57,320
[SPEAKER_00]: It is like a king who has defeated his enemies.

280
00:20:57,400 --> 00:21:01,201
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees no danger from his enemies in any direction.

281
00:21:01,921 --> 00:21:09,403
[SPEAKER_00]: In that same way, a monk thus accomplished in virtue sees no danger in any direction in regards to their virtuous restraint.

282
00:21:10,703 --> 00:21:15,825
[SPEAKER_00]: When they have this aggregate, this domain of noble virtue,

283
00:21:16,705 --> 00:21:22,730
[SPEAKER_00]: The monk who has ceased to depend on the external circumstances, external authority, external definitions, external rules, external requirements,

284
00:21:41,614 --> 00:21:42,354
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

285
00:21:46,437 --> 00:21:48,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing right is right, wrong is wrong.

286
00:21:48,298 --> 00:21:49,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome.

287
00:21:49,639 --> 00:21:52,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Means knowing, lust, aversion, delusion sufficiently enough.

288
00:21:53,888 --> 00:21:55,669
[SPEAKER_01]: To always see it and not act out of it.

289
00:21:56,189 --> 00:21:58,270
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice that, you will become accomplished in that.

290
00:21:58,730 --> 00:22:07,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, doesn't matter the circumstance, doesn't matter what quarter the circumstances arrive at you, from what direction, there is no danger, because you're not depending on that.

291
00:22:08,193 --> 00:22:14,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody comes and starts questioning the certain interpretation of a letter, of a rule, it doesn't matter, because that's not what your virtue was based on.

292
00:22:15,315 --> 00:22:19,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It was based on you not doing anything out of intention or greed, aversion, delusion.

293
00:22:20,739 --> 00:22:29,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if somebody says, look, all these rules were actually wrong, they were written by somebody later, inserted there, they're not right, makes no difference.

294
00:22:30,461 --> 00:22:36,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would actually know that they were right, even if somebody did edit them rightly, because they're according to the standard of dhamma.

295
00:22:36,683 --> 00:22:40,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Standard of dispassion, disenchantment, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

296
00:22:42,264 --> 00:22:49,926
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you put the letter first, the external authority of Sīla Bhārata Paramāsa first, you will not see this.

297
00:22:51,479 --> 00:22:55,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're refusing to take responsibility for it, and allow it to become internal, because it's unpleasant.

298
00:22:56,483 --> 00:23:05,190
[SPEAKER_01]: The weight of it is unpleasant, the weight of responsibility is unpleasant, the pressure is unpleasant, the liability to doubt is unpleasant.

299
00:23:05,210 --> 00:23:05,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to endure it.

300
00:23:06,431 --> 00:23:14,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing of samādhi is unpleasant.

301
00:23:16,459 --> 00:23:16,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And same.

302
00:23:17,419 --> 00:23:19,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Eight precepts for a layperson, same.

303
00:23:21,013 --> 00:23:45,343
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why often the Buddha would not even overly define things, because if the mind isn't... hasn't strayed from the right context of non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, recognizes that state of mind, doesn't act out of it regardless of what the action might be, you don't need extra and extra layers of definitions and words and interpretations and meanings, because the meaning is there, the context is there, it's clear.

304
00:23:46,883 --> 00:23:50,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why practically eight precepts are modern enough, certainly for anāgāmī.

305
00:23:53,636 --> 00:23:58,765
[SPEAKER_01]: In a lay setting, for a monk, yeah, you'll be an arahant, accomplished to the same virtue, the same thread.

306
00:24:01,009 --> 00:24:03,774
[SPEAKER_00]: I like the way it's said, like, that he is...

307
00:24:04,835 --> 00:24:11,358
[SPEAKER_00]: He who is accomplished or attained in possesses the aggregate of virtue, the aggregate of virtue.

308
00:24:12,059 --> 00:24:13,319
[SPEAKER_00]: He has created this aggregate.

309
00:24:13,399 --> 00:24:14,660
[SPEAKER_01]: He created an aggregate, yeah.

310
00:24:15,140 --> 00:24:16,801
[SPEAKER_00]: That now he can recognize.

311
00:24:16,941 --> 00:24:26,646
[SPEAKER_01]: He can recognize and he's encouraged to recollect and dwell because it will re-inspire confidence and sense of safety and gladness that will then bring samādhi and so on to fulfillment.

312
00:24:27,526 --> 00:24:30,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And clear understanding of what's... what's what.

313
00:24:31,108 --> 00:24:32,109
[SPEAKER_01]: But not without it.

314
00:24:33,029 --> 00:24:35,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is, again, that is the entire practice.

315
00:24:37,291 --> 00:24:39,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Your samādhi comes out of it, your pañña comes out of it.

316
00:24:40,493 --> 00:24:42,194
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you do it, the more you perfect it.

317
00:24:42,214 --> 00:24:46,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Like greed, aversion, delusion, removed from physical, verbal and mental.

318
00:24:47,097 --> 00:24:47,817
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what dhamma is.

319
00:24:49,298 --> 00:24:51,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, start on the physical level.

320
00:24:52,440 --> 00:24:56,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is your samādhi, that is your pañña, that is your practice towards sotāpatti and higher.

321
00:25:00,452 --> 00:25:01,013
[SPEAKER_00]: You never give...

322
00:25:01,033 --> 00:25:02,194
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not found elsewhere.

323
00:25:02,374 --> 00:25:03,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not found after.

324
00:25:03,796 --> 00:25:04,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there and then.

325
00:25:05,217 --> 00:25:08,721
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not done for just a short period of time and then... No.

326
00:25:09,041 --> 00:25:09,582
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying.

327
00:25:09,662 --> 00:25:16,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It's better to even, like, if you, if you, oh, hate priesthood too much, as a layperson, take the five priesthoods for the rest of your life.

328
00:25:17,851 --> 00:25:18,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But not for the weekend.

329
00:25:20,384 --> 00:25:25,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Because means you never really allow... you never really consider changing the order of what's important to you.

330
00:25:25,428 --> 00:25:26,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is important to you.

331
00:25:27,430 --> 00:25:30,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So now you realize that, yeah, precepts are good and maybe I should do something about it.

332
00:25:30,752 --> 00:25:33,835
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you only do it like for two or three days or five days.

333
00:25:33,915 --> 00:25:43,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, oh, you're just sort of... toying with it, while at the same time, refusing to allow the actual... no, sensuality to be put second.

334
00:25:44,724 --> 00:25:47,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And my precepts will now be first for the rest of my life.

335
00:25:49,343 --> 00:25:50,624
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's important.

336
00:25:50,924 --> 00:25:51,385
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is.

337
00:25:51,665 --> 00:25:53,926
[SPEAKER_01]: He undertakes the training of virtue.

338
00:25:55,548 --> 00:25:57,209
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to take it in that ultimate sense.

339
00:25:57,849 --> 00:26:01,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Not this conditional, I'll do it a bit until I get meditation experience or something.

340
00:26:01,852 --> 00:26:02,112
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

341
00:26:03,734 --> 00:26:06,315
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, it's better to have five precepts, but for the rest of your life.

342
00:26:07,116 --> 00:26:14,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Than to, like, no precepts and then super austere level of celibacy for ten days and then, boom, no precepts at all.

343
00:26:17,393 --> 00:26:18,815
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not accomplishing anything, really.

344
00:26:18,876 --> 00:26:22,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I mean, you're obviously trying, so that's a good thing, I suppose.

345
00:26:23,222 --> 00:26:31,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to recognize the gradual training starts gradually and starts in that responsible sense, but absolutely non-negotiable sense.

346
00:26:32,916 --> 00:26:39,062
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, okay, so what can I sustain and what am I able to... can I make this determination for the rest of my life?

347
00:26:39,923 --> 00:26:45,868
[SPEAKER_01]: To not kill, cheat, lie, steal, misconduct, take intoxicants.

348
00:26:46,649 --> 00:26:49,371
[SPEAKER_01]: And if just that thought scares you, oh, I can't do this...

349
00:26:51,338 --> 00:26:56,321
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to do that first, before you can even consider direction of any practice whatsoever.

350
00:26:56,661 --> 00:26:57,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the practice.

351
00:26:58,182 --> 00:27:00,324
[SPEAKER_01]: You considering that, is the practice.

352
00:27:01,044 --> 00:27:08,850
[SPEAKER_01]: You making yourself being able to determine to keep the five precepts for the rest of your life, or eight precepts for the rest of your life, is the beginning of your accomplishment in virtue.

353
00:27:11,195 --> 00:27:15,476
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't come up, come, come, like, it won't result on its own, somehow.

354
00:27:15,977 --> 00:27:24,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you got a, you know, a very, very, very potent meditation method, that will now, if you adhere to it, just create this virtue for you.

355
00:27:25,299 --> 00:27:30,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Another sutta called the restraint of the patimokkha.

356
00:27:32,882 --> 00:27:36,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the blessed one and asked him,

357
00:27:38,526 --> 00:27:48,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Sir, it would be good if the Blessed One would teach me the dhamma in brief, so that, having heard the dhamma from the Blessed One, I might dwell alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent and resolute.

358
00:27:52,198 --> 00:27:57,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha replies, in that case, we could purify the very beginning of wholesome states.

359
00:27:59,514 --> 00:28:01,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is the beginning of wholesome states?

360
00:28:02,076 --> 00:28:14,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, Bhikkhu, you dwell restrained by the restraint of the patimokkha, accomplished in good conduct and proper resort, seeing danger in the slightest false, having undertaken the training rules, training them.

361
00:28:15,668 --> 00:28:18,070
[SPEAKER_00]: When, Bhikkhu, you dwell restrained in such a way,

362
00:28:20,483 --> 00:28:30,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the danger in the slightest fault, then, based upon that virtue, established upon virtue, you should develop the four establishments of mindfulness.

363
00:28:31,839 --> 00:28:32,680
[SPEAKER_00]: The satipaṭṭhāna.

364
00:28:34,611 --> 00:28:35,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Not before that.

365
00:28:35,571 --> 00:28:36,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Not before that, yeah.

366
00:28:36,472 --> 00:28:39,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're doing your mindfulness before you even accomplish yourself in precepts.

367
00:28:40,873 --> 00:28:41,354
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just...

368
00:28:41,774 --> 00:28:42,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Goes against...

369
00:28:42,314 --> 00:28:43,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Putting the cart in front of a horse.

370
00:28:43,755 --> 00:28:43,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

371
00:28:44,755 --> 00:28:45,775
[SPEAKER_00]: And expecting it to move.

372
00:28:48,196 --> 00:28:50,677
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no way to have that type of a discernment.

373
00:28:50,697 --> 00:28:50,837
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

374
00:28:50,857 --> 00:28:51,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the same.

375
00:28:51,418 --> 00:28:53,178
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's mindfulness, samādhi.

376
00:28:53,919 --> 00:29:01,782
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same development, same thing that comes on the basis of established, uncompromised, unbroken virtue.

377
00:29:03,693 --> 00:29:04,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Uncompromised.

378
00:29:04,474 --> 00:29:04,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:29:04,814 --> 00:29:06,956
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you uncompromise?

380
00:29:06,976 --> 00:29:11,420
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you not compromise your virtue?

381
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:23,111
[SPEAKER_01]: By overly defining your rules and sort of, you know, proliferating aspects or by simply not accepting any action that your mind offers you out of greed, aversion, delusion?

382
00:29:23,311 --> 00:29:23,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that.

383
00:29:24,052 --> 00:29:24,752
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that.

384
00:29:24,932 --> 00:29:25,453
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that.

385
00:29:26,665 --> 00:29:27,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I acting out of hindrances?

386
00:29:28,287 --> 00:29:29,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I acting out of doubt?

387
00:29:29,948 --> 00:29:31,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I acting out of restlessness?

388
00:29:31,709 --> 00:29:36,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I acting out of sensuality, anger, sloth and laziness?

389
00:29:37,554 --> 00:29:38,115
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unwholesome.

390
00:29:38,375 --> 00:29:40,577
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't do it, I am accomplishing virtue.

391
00:29:41,437 --> 00:29:44,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will be abandoning hindrances as well, which is what samādhi is.

392
00:29:45,961 --> 00:29:47,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Not acting out of unwholesome states.

393
00:29:47,562 --> 00:29:49,824
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we speak about it so, so, so often.

394
00:29:50,205 --> 00:29:55,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that is contained between you and your relationship with your own virtue or lack of it.

395
00:29:56,833 --> 00:30:00,274
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say lack of virtue, it doesn't mean you don't keep the precepts.

396
00:30:00,674 --> 00:30:07,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you keep them, but in that external, syllabar paramāsa sense, and you're not internally becoming accomplished in those rules.

397
00:30:09,197 --> 00:30:12,838
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you're not internalizing criteria for unwholesome that you're avoiding.

398
00:30:13,418 --> 00:30:17,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just adhering to the definition through your intellect, tradition, authority and so on.

399
00:30:18,700 --> 00:30:24,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're not... you don't have the clarity that comes from accomplishment in virtue,

400
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:28,001
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be mindful of the body.

401
00:30:28,361 --> 00:30:28,781
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

402
00:30:28,801 --> 00:30:30,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mindfulness will be external.

403
00:30:31,102 --> 00:30:36,145
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your responsibility for your virtues is external.

404
00:30:36,185 --> 00:30:40,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you do mindfulness, you'll be doing it on the level of objects of your senses, which is not mindfulness.

405
00:30:40,927 --> 00:30:41,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Duty.

406
00:30:41,088 --> 00:30:42,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Will be like a duty.

407
00:30:42,108 --> 00:30:44,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what meditation technique is for people.

408
00:30:44,589 --> 00:30:47,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It's often taken in a sense of another external duty that I must do.

409
00:30:48,331 --> 00:30:49,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And it certainly

410
00:30:49,952 --> 00:30:52,013
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always taken on the level of sense objects.

411
00:30:52,433 --> 00:30:55,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Observations, nimittas and whatever else.

412
00:30:55,714 --> 00:30:56,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Movements.

413
00:30:56,254 --> 00:30:57,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Movements, motions.

414
00:30:57,875 --> 00:30:59,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It's never taken as the internal... Recitations.

415
00:31:00,336 --> 00:31:01,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Recitations, mantras.

416
00:31:02,416 --> 00:31:03,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Visualizations.

417
00:31:03,077 --> 00:31:05,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Visualizations, yeah.

418
00:31:05,597 --> 00:31:06,678
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's taken there.

419
00:31:07,138 --> 00:31:14,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no basis for mindfulness, no sufficient basis for mindfulness to be taken on the right peripheral, sufficiently peripheral context.

420
00:31:15,185 --> 00:31:25,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what happens then, is just you take it where your mind is, and your mind is out there on the external things, because you want to avoid doubt and all these things, so you're just adhering to external things with a sense of duty.

421
00:31:25,994 --> 00:31:27,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīlabhātaparamāsa.

422
00:31:30,217 --> 00:31:33,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And then your meditation becomes on the level of that sīlabhātaparamāsa.

423
00:31:34,971 --> 00:31:37,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And then your discernment is on that level.

424
00:31:38,372 --> 00:31:39,552
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's not a discernment.

425
00:31:40,093 --> 00:31:45,274
[SPEAKER_01]: You're discerning things more on the level of sense objects without having the right context, the right basis for it.

426
00:31:47,035 --> 00:31:50,436
[SPEAKER_01]: All because you haven't allowed yourself to become accomplished in that virtue correctly.

427
00:31:50,896 --> 00:31:51,836
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't taken it up.

428
00:31:52,277 --> 00:31:56,498
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't accepted the weight of responsibility for it and endured it.

